  "notes": "Piano-led orchestral pop: about 95 straight with the backbeat on 2&4 and \"a continuous 16th piano ostinato\", which is the identity — \"the *piano riff is the hook* — a continuous 16th-note broken-chord figure that never stops\". That is why the countermelody runs as an `arp` at the highest ratio in this batch with a 1/16 echo, and why the melody above it is stepwise with an urgent repeated-note contour. Three drum deltas are quoted: \"kick 1 and 3\", \"8th hat\", and the arrangement device that is really a drum fact — \"the drums dropping out entirely for whole sections\" — which is `dropOutBeats` at 0.95 over 8 beats and a fill schedule pushed out to 16 bars; the kit was \"recorded with a small band and edited into 'starts and stops'\", so `humanize` keeps a live velocity spread against a tight grid. Harmony is B major running diatonic I–V–vi–IV-family loops over a moving left-hand bass, and the bass \"doubles the piano's left hand, root motion only\" — `independent_riff` at 0.95. ⚠ `confidence` is medium because the research rates it high-medium. ⚠ The **INFERRED** parameters are all drum probabilities and the melody numbers; \"stepwise with an urgent repeated-note contour\" is a direction, and a step share of 0.84 with a 0.5 `straightEighthsProb` is this author's reading of it.",
